A deadlock is a situation where two or more processes are unable to proceed because each is waiting for a resource held by another process.

🔹 Example

Process P1 holds Resource R1 and waits for R2

Process P2 holds Resource R2 and waits for R1
➡️ This circular waiting results in a deadlock

🔹 Necessary Conditions for Deadlock

Deadlock occurs only if all four conditions hold:

Mutual Exclusion – Resources cannot be shared

Hold and Wait – A process holds at least one resource while waiting for others

No Preemption – Resources cannot be forcibly taken from a process

Circular Wait – A circular chain of processes exists where each waits for a resource held by the next

🔹 Deadlock Handling Methods
✅ Prevention

Eliminate at least one of the necessary conditions

✅ Avoidance

System ensures it never enters an unsafe state (e.g., Banker’s algorithm)

✅ Detection and Recovery

Detect deadlock and recover by terminating processes or preempting resources

✅ Ignore (Ostrich Approach)

Assume deadlocks are rare and ignore them

🔹 Safe State vs Unsafe State

Safe state → System can allocate resources without causing deadlock

Unsafe state → Deadlock may occur

🔹 Advantages of Handling Deadlock

Improved system stability

Better resource utilization

Increased performance
